      >, via Wikimedia Commons (resized).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What are the best overnight hikes in the Blue Mountains?</h2>
  <p>
    "Best" depends on your fitness and how comfortable you are on steep, uneven
    track. The routes below are all inside Blue Mountains National Park and are
    listed roughly from easiest to hardest, from a night next to the car to a
    serious three-day walk.
  </p>
  <figure class="guide-figure">
    <div class="guide-table-wrap">
      <table class="guide-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Route</th><th>Distance</th><th>Booking needed?</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Perrys Lookdown campground (a test night by the car)</td>
            <td>Beside the Perrys Lookdown car park, almost no walking</td>
            <td>No, first come first served, one night limit</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Ingar campground (Woodford / Wentworth Falls area)</td>
            <td>About 5km each way on fire trail from Murphys Rd, Woodford (about 10.5km from Woodford station)</td>
            <td>Yes, online</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Perrys Lookdown to Blue Gum Forest / Acacia Flat</td>
            <td>4km return, grade 4 (hard), about 2h down and 2h up</td>
            <td>Acacia Flat campground: yes, online</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Kedumba River Crossing campground</td>
            <td>9km one-way via Kedumba Pass from Wentworth Falls</td>
            <td>Yes, online</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Six Foot Track (Katoomba to Binda Bush cabins)</td>
            <td>42km one-way, usually 3 days</td>
            <td
              >No park booking for camping; cabins/transport arranged separately</td
            >
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      Distances and grades from NSW National Parks and hiker trip reports.
      Check current alerts before you go; tracks and campgrounds close for
      weather, fire and maintenance.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Which Blue Mountains overnight hike should I do first?</h2>
  <p>
    If you've never camped out of a pack before, work up in steps rather than
    jumping straight to the hardest walk:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Perrys Lookdown campground.</strong> It's basically a car-park
      campsite, so it's a good low-stakes test night for your tent, mat and
      sleeping bag. Go midweek or off-peak, since it's first come, first served.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ingar campground.</strong> A gentle fire-trail walk, easy to
      navigate, reachable by train, with a swimming hole at the end.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Acacia Flat via Perrys Lookdown.</strong> Short, but a steep
      descent and a hard climb back out with a full pack.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Kedumba River Crossing.</strong> Longer, with a long, steep climb
      out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The Six Foot Track.</strong> Three days, once you have a few
      nights behind you.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Ingar campground: an easy first walk-in overnighter</h2>
  <p>
    <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/camping-and-accommodation/campgrounds/ingar-campground"
      rel="noopener">Ingar campground</a
    > sits south of Woodford and Wentworth Falls. It's about 5km each way on
    fire trail from Murphys Rd, Woodford, or about 10.5km in from Woodford
    station if you come by train. The trail is easy to follow and much gentler
    than Perrys, though there's still around 500m of up and down over the round
    trip and an ankle-deep crossing of Bedford Creek about 2.6km in. The camp has
    eight unmarked tent sites, toilets and picnic tables, and a waterhole people
    swim in. There's no tap water, so treat water from the waterhole (tablets
    are the cheapest option) or carry your own. It needs an online booking.
  </p>

  <h2>Perrys Lookdown to Blue Gum Forest and Acacia Flat</h2>
  <p>
    This is the route most Blue Mountains guides point beginners to because it
    is short on paper. NPWS lists it as a 4km return, grade 4 walk of 3 to 4
    hours, dropping steeply from the Perrys Lookdown car park
    near Blackheath through a cliff line into the Grose Valley to reach the
    historic <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/things-to-do/walking-tracks/perrys-lookdown-to-blue-gum-forest-walking-track"
      rel="noopener">Blue Gum Forest</a
    >.
  </p>
  <p>
    "Grade 4" on the NPWS scale means a steep, challenging track with many
    steps, and it feels harder again with an overnight pack on the way back up.
    Hikers typically take about 2 hours down and 2 hours back up. Leave early
    to avoid the heat, and trekking poles help a lot on the steps. Acacia Flat campground, near the forest, needs an <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/camping-and-accommodation/campgrounds/acacia-flat-campground"
      rel="noopener">online booking</a
    > and charges a small booking fee rather than a nightly camping fee. There is
    no tap water, but you can treat creek water (Orang Utan Gully, at the
    southern end of the camp, is the usual pick); carry a backup supply in case
    it's low. Only fuel stoves are permitted there, not open campfires. Expect
    leeches after rain, and no phone reception in the valley.
  </p>
  <p>
    Perrys Lookdown campground itself, right at the car park, cannot be
    reserved and is limited to one night's stay. It makes a good first night:
    sleep near the car, then walk down to Acacia Flat on a later trip.
  </p>

  <h2>Kedumba River Crossing: a step up</h2>
  <p>
    Kedumba River Crossing campground is the longer, harder option near
    Wentworth Falls, and needs an online booking. It's about 9km one-way down the
    steep Kedumba Pass from Wentworth Falls, with 20 sites beside the river.
    NPWS itself describes the climb back out as "extremely steep and
    challenging" and says it takes 3 to 4 hours, so this one suits hikers who
    already have an overnight trip behind them rather than a first attempt.
    Hikers report about 3 hours in and 3.5 hours out on steep fire trail, and
    you may have the camp to yourself midweek in winter. Landslips have closed
    the western approaches to Mount Solitary, so don't plan a loop that way
    without checking current alerts.
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    Allow 3 to 4 hours for the Kedumba Pass climb out of the valley. Treat that
    as the normal time for your return leg, not a worst case.
  </blockquote>

  <h2>Is the Six Foot Track a good overnight hike for beginners?</h2>
  <p>
    Not as a first trip. The Six Foot Track runs 42km one-way from Katoomba and
    is usually walked over three days, which means two nights of self-supported
    camping and carrying more food and water than a single overnight hike. It is
    worth knowing about once you have one or two shorter overnight hikes under
    your belt. Currently the track ends at Binda Bush cabins rather than
    continuing to Jenolan Caves, because the <a
      href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/things-to-do/walking-tracks/six-foot-track"
      rel="noopener">Jenolan Karst Conservation Reserve is closed</a
    > until at least the end of 2026; check the NPWS alerts on that page before you plan transport, since there
    is no public transport to or from Binda Bush cabins.
  </p>

  <h2>What are the fire season rules for Blue Mountains overnight hikes?</h2>
  <p>
    NSW's statewide Bush Fire Danger Period runs from 1 October, and it is the
    backdrop for almost every spring and summer overnight hike in the Blue
    Mountains. During that period, and especially on a day the NSW Rural Fire
    Service declares a Total Fire Ban, the rules tighten:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      On a Total Fire Ban day you cannot light, maintain or use an open fire,
      and solid-fuel cooking (wood, charcoal, heat-bead barbecues) is banned
      outright, according to the <a
        href="https://www.rfs.nsw.gov.au/fire-information/fdr-and-tobans/total-fire-ban-rules"
        rel="noopener">NSW RFS Total Fire Ban rules</a
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      The RFS gas exemption is written for homes and approved picnic areas, not
      backcountry campsites, so don't assume your gas stove is fine on a Total
      Fire Ban day.
    </li>
    <li>
      NPWS also declares its own park fire bans, separate from RFS Total Fire
      Bans. These can ban all campfires and wood or solid-fuel (hexi) stoves
      across the park for months, which catches out people with cheap wood or
      hexi stoves.
    </li>
    <li>
      NPWS can and does close individual tracks and campgrounds for fire risk
      independently of a Total Fire Ban, so a park being open last week doesn't
      mean it's open this week.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because this can change day to day, treat it as a final check rather than
    something to plan around weeks out: look up the specific track's NPWS alerts
    page and the current NSW RFS fire ban status the afternoon before you leave,
    and have a fallback plan if either says no. Our <a
      href="/guides/hiking-stove-guide/">hiking stove guide</a
    > covers stove types and fuel in more depth if you're still choosing gear.
  </p>

  <h2>How do I book an NSW National Parks campsite for an overnight hike?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Most Blue Mountains walk-in campgrounds, including Acacia Flat, Ingar and
      Kedumba River Crossing, are booked online through the <a
        href="https://www.nationalparks.nsw.gov.au/camping-and-accommodation/camping-in-national-parks"
        rel="noopener">NSW National Parks booking system</a
      >, with a small booking fee rather than a nightly camping charge.
    </li>
    <li>
      You can also book by phone through the National Parks Contact Centre on
      1300 072 757 (7am to 7pm daily).
    </li>
    <li>
      A handful of basic sites, like Perrys Lookdown campground, are first come,
      first served and cannot be reserved at all.
    </li>
    <li>
      Tell someone your plans and when you expect to be back; there's no phone
      reception in the valley.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What gear do you need for an overnight hike in the Blue Mountains?</h2>
  <p>
    The routes above don't need anything exotic, but the steep, rocky descents
    reward a pack that actually fits and gear you've tested before you're 4km
    from the car with a sore back. Our <a
      href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/">overnight hike packing list</a
    > covers the full kit list, and if you're still choosing a sleeping system, start
    with <a href="/guides/sleeping-bag-temperature-ratings/"
      >sleeping bag temperature ratings</a
    > and <a href="/guides/sleeping-mat-r-value/">sleeping mat R-value</a>,
    since Blue Mountains nights get cold even when Sydney doesn't. Our <a
      href="/#kits">beginner hiking kits</a
    > bundle a pack, sleep system and shelter together, or use <a
      href="/build-my-kit/">Build My Kit</a
    > to fill gaps in gear you already own.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Start small: a night at Perrys Lookdown campground or an easy walk into
    Ingar, then take on the steep walk down to Acacia Flat once your gear and
    legs are sorted. Book your campsite online ahead of time, save the Six Foot Track for once
    you've done a night or two, and always check NPWS alerts and the NSW RFS
    fire ban status the day before you leave, since both can change the plan at
    short notice.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary
        >What is the best overnight hike near Sydney for beginners?</summary
      >
      <p>
        In the Blue Mountains, Ingar campground is a gentle first walk-in trip:
        about 5km each way on fire trail from Woodford, and reachable by train.
        Perrys Lookdown to Acacia Flat is shorter (4km return) but much steeper,
        so it suits a second trip. For a no-walking test night, try Perrys
        Lookdown campground by the car park.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >Do I need to book a campsite for an overnight hike in the Blue
        Mountains?</summary
      >
      <p>
        For most walk-in NPWS campgrounds, including Acacia Flat, Ingar and
        Kedumba River Crossing, yes, you book online in advance. A few basic
        sites, like Perrys Lookdown, are first come, first served.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How long is the Six Foot Track?</summary>
      <p>
        The Six Foot Track is 42km one-way and is usually walked over three
        days. It currently finishes at Binda Bush cabins rather than Jenolan
        Caves while the Jenolan Karst Conservation Reserve is closed.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >Can I use a gas stove during a total fire ban in the Blue Mountains?</summary
      >
      <p>
        Be cautious. NSW RFS total fire ban rules ban open fires and solid-fuel
        cooking outright, and its published gas barbecue exemption is written
        for private dwellings and approved picnic areas, not backcountry
        campsites. Check the current RFS fire ban status and the track's NPWS
        alerts before you rely on cooking with any stove.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>When does NSW's fire season affect Blue Mountains hikes?</summary
      >
      <p>
        The statewide Bush Fire Danger Period runs from 1 October. Total Fire
        Bans can be declared on individual high-risk days within and sometimes
        outside that period, and NPWS park fire bans can ban wood and
        solid-fuel stoves for months, so check status close to your trip date.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is there water at Acacia Flat campground?</summary>
      <p>
        There's no tap water, but you can treat creek water from Orang Utan
        Gully at the southern end of the camp with tablets, a filter or by
        boiling. Carry a backup supply in case it's low.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Is Jenolan Caves currently open?</summary>
      <p>
        No. The Jenolan Karst Conservation Reserve is closed until at least the
        end of 2026, with reopening targeted for early 2027, and the Six Foot
        Track ends at Binda Bush cabins instead of continuing to
        the caves. Recheck the NPWS track page before planning transport, as
        this may change.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
